# Line-ending policy for Horizon — this file is canonical.
#
# The repo is developed on Windows and deployed on Linux. Git for Windows ships
# with core.autocrlf=true in its system config, so contributors get CRLF working
# trees whether they asked for one or not. Attributes take precedence over that
# config, which is why the policy lives here and not in a setup instruction
# somebody has to remember: LF in the index, LF on checkout, on every machine.
#
# This was safe to add because the index was already 100% LF when it landed
# (verified with `git ls-files --eol`: 90 text files, zero i/crlf). It therefore
# changed no tracked content and required no `git add --renormalize`.
#
# Concretely it fixes: gofmt reporting every .go file as unformatted because it
# read CRLF, which would otherwise fail the `go fmt` diff check in CI.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Images. Marked binary so Git never applies EOL conversion and never tries to
# diff or merge them as text. Only PNGs are committed today; the rest are listed
# so a future asset lands correctly the first time.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary

# Signing material and certificates. All of these are ignored by
# mobile/.gitignore — the declaration is here so that a deliberate force-add
# cannot silently corrupt one through line-ending conversion.
*.jks binary
*.p8 binary
*.p12 binary
*.keystore binary
*.mobileprovision binary
